[[:db/retract 17592186124953 :question/answer-fields 17592186122318] [:db/retract 17592186125221 :question/answer-fields 17592186123926] {:db/id 17592186124452 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c11b4e02-4fa9-528c-867b-1ff09ac00c10" "question-88f91bc6-5d92-5d89-9639-1db1ee91906d" "question-7e76204f-7492-5638-91de-b17ed7af74ee"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124453 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-15e24e7e-a45b-5130-9771-640280afca4e" "question-a678f4db-f59f-58b6-9d8f-ba8fadd30c17"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124558 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-cdbbe1e3-c4b9-56df-b019-b8be69ef537e" "question-6e200150-12cc-5141-a4cc-a34826db19ce"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124457 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9df4444a-990b-5f68-bec9-ae4f2c45450b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c11b4e02-4fa9-528c-867b-1ff09ac00c10" :question/id #uuid "c11b4e02-4fa9-528c-867b-1ff09ac00c10" :question/math-academy-id "q-74911" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (4,-4,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z}=$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(4,-4,-2)$ does not cause the  denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(4,-4,-2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (4,-4,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z} & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{4-(-4)}}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{8}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{-2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f486c765-ecd7-5a87-8f26-635e2f270bec"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f486c765-ecd7-5a87-8f26-635e2f270bec" :answer-field/id #uuid "f486c765-ecd7-5a87-8f26-635e2f270bec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-23db4813-aed1-5501-99cf-12aa83f3383d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-23db4813-aed1-5501-99cf-12aa83f3383d" "answer-e3f4f9e0-fb6f-53dd-a9eb-91c9e707abd4" "answer-1e050b6b-4c71-5b1d-95f2-c237914af9e0" "answer-a7f9219d-acd8-5505-9300-47d9ac52607b" "answer-4c47b885-90cb-59cf-b936-3007bf1a5ce1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23db4813-aed1-5501-99cf-12aa83f3383d" :answer/id #uuid "23db4813-aed1-5501-99cf-12aa83f3383d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e3f4f9e0-fb6f-53dd-a9eb-91c9e707abd4" :answer/id #uuid "e3f4f9e0-fb6f-53dd-a9eb-91c9e707abd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1e050b6b-4c71-5b1d-95f2-c237914af9e0" :answer/id #uuid "1e050b6b-4c71-5b1d-95f2-c237914af9e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7f9219d-acd8-5505-9300-47d9ac52607b" :answer/id #uuid "a7f9219d-acd8-5505-9300-47d9ac52607b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c47b885-90cb-59cf-b936-3007bf1a5ce1" :answer/id #uuid "4c47b885-90cb-59cf-b936-3007bf1a5ce1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-88f91bc6-5d92-5d89-9639-1db1ee91906d" :question/id #uuid "88f91bc6-5d92-5d89-9639-1db1ee91906d" :question/math-academy-id "q-74908" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt{xy}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the expression under the radical   is negative. However, for the point $(-2,-2)$ the product $x\\cdot y$ is not negative, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-2,-2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2,-2)}\\sqrt{xy}=\\sqrt{(-2)\\cdot (-2)}=\\sqrt{4}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c964af35-b530-511c-ba6c-a0a70d2a6c2e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c964af35-b530-511c-ba6c-a0a70d2a6c2e" :answer-field/id #uuid "c964af35-b530-511c-ba6c-a0a70d2a6c2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5e0718a8-d111-53b4-8167-8361933440ad" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8579266b-a01b-54d9-9032-9c94a818a557" "answer-5e0718a8-d111-53b4-8167-8361933440ad" "answer-566a1241-e2de-561e-9daf-82cc2652adff" "answer-034d1791-5b87-5fc0-88f4-c35a94cf6f24" "answer-705005f1-4e76-51ab-bd86-bbc4756b8e21"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8579266b-a01b-54d9-9032-9c94a818a557" :answer/id #uuid "8579266b-a01b-54d9-9032-9c94a818a557" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e0718a8-d111-53b4-8167-8361933440ad" :answer/id #uuid "5e0718a8-d111-53b4-8167-8361933440ad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-566a1241-e2de-561e-9daf-82cc2652adff" :answer/id #uuid "566a1241-e2de-561e-9daf-82cc2652adff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-034d1791-5b87-5fc0-88f4-c35a94cf6f24" :answer/id #uuid "034d1791-5b87-5fc0-88f4-c35a94cf6f24" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-705005f1-4e76-51ab-bd86-bbc4756b8e21" :answer/id #uuid "705005f1-4e76-51ab-bd86-bbc4756b8e21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7e76204f-7492-5638-91de-b17ed7af74ee" :question/id #uuid "7e76204f-7492-5638-91de-b17ed7af74ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-129323" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{x(xy-1)}{y} & =\\frac{2(2\\cdot 1-1)}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1)}{1} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-65295b29-6d49-5c32-a7c7-3761c22ba9ce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-65295b29-6d49-5c32-a7c7-3761c22ba9ce" :answer-field/id #uuid "65295b29-6d49-5c32-a7c7-3761c22ba9ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-416f53e7-0424-5030-ac7c-3c49b5372569" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5aceb8dd-e06d-5296-8078-5ae4cc750061" "answer-b61fdeeb-ac42-57e7-8628-5828106b4cdc" "answer-416f53e7-0424-5030-ac7c-3c49b5372569" "answer-83965ef5-938e-5708-bac6-916fb84faf46" "answer-696c1dec-c738-5e33-a673-9e3ab3c19a5f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5aceb8dd-e06d-5296-8078-5ae4cc750061" :answer/id #uuid "5aceb8dd-e06d-5296-8078-5ae4cc750061" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b61fdeeb-ac42-57e7-8628-5828106b4cdc" :answer/id #uuid "b61fdeeb-ac42-57e7-8628-5828106b4cdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-416f53e7-0424-5030-ac7c-3c49b5372569" :answer/id #uuid "416f53e7-0424-5030-ac7c-3c49b5372569" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83965ef5-938e-5708-bac6-916fb84faf46" :answer/id #uuid "83965ef5-938e-5708-bac6-916fb84faf46" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-696c1dec-c738-5e33-a673-9e3ab3c19a5f" :answer/id #uuid "696c1dec-c738-5e33-a673-9e3ab3c19a5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-15e24e7e-a45b-5130-9771-640280afca4e" :question/id #uuid "15e24e7e-a45b-5130-9771-640280afca4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74953" :question/problem "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{1+(-1)}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+z)+y(x+z)}{x+z} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x+z)}{x+z} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}x+y & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-050f7890-b08d-5d96-a6f3-aa1024a4d86d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-050f7890-b08d-5d96-a6f3-aa1024a4d86d" :answer-field/id #uuid "050f7890-b08d-5d96-a6f3-aa1024a4d86d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-40aa43f3-5052-52eb-9041-2ff9b581bcb5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fcc96a06-c359-5bf6-a8b3-a2cef416b538" "answer-c65ea214-6d95-58fa-a971-d2654e7a972b" "answer-40aa43f3-5052-52eb-9041-2ff9b581bcb5" "answer-e1fb3b67-eae3-54a3-a2f2-8864cb03f007" "answer-4b176e8d-dda0-56b4-82b1-b56ebe13e094"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcc96a06-c359-5bf6-a8b3-a2cef416b538" :answer/id #uuid "fcc96a06-c359-5bf6-a8b3-a2cef416b538" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c65ea214-6d95-58fa-a971-d2654e7a972b" :answer/id #uuid "c65ea214-6d95-58fa-a971-d2654e7a972b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40aa43f3-5052-52eb-9041-2ff9b581bcb5" :answer/id #uuid "40aa43f3-5052-52eb-9041-2ff9b581bcb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1fb3b67-eae3-54a3-a2f2-8864cb03f007" :answer/id #uuid "e1fb3b67-eae3-54a3-a2f2-8864cb03f007" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b176e8d-dda0-56b4-82b1-b56ebe13e094" :answer/id #uuid "4b176e8d-dda0-56b4-82b1-b56ebe13e094" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125220 :question/problem "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z}=\\frac{{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{{2}^{3}-{2}^{2}\\cdot 2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{z}^{3}-{y}^{2}z} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{{z}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{z({z}^{2}-{y}^{2})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{1}{z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,2,2)}\\frac{1}{z} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186123920 :answer-field/correct 17592186123921 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-a678f4db-f59f-58b6-9d8f-ba8fadd30c17" :question/id #uuid "a678f4db-f59f-58b6-9d8f-ba8fadd30c17" :question/math-academy-id "q-129336" :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y} & =\\frac{{1}^{3}+2(1)^{2}(-1)+1(-1)^{2}}{1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-2+1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{x+y} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x({x}^{2}+2xy+{y}^{2})}{x+y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+y)(x+y)}{x+y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+y)\\cancel{(x+y)}}{\\cancel{x+y}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}x(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,-1)}x(x+y) & =1(1-1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8116b6c2-1cab-5c9a-af1a-53984a742595"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8116b6c2-1cab-5c9a-af1a-53984a742595" :answer-field/id #uuid "8116b6c2-1cab-5c9a-af1a-53984a742595" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-baaec9e1-8b5e-5f7f-8ef8-59b96e26a955" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d6a58b3c-76e3-54b6-ab35-4bf1be82b8db" "answer-289a71e6-833a-5782-99bf-9eaaf974a1d9" "answer-baaec9e1-8b5e-5f7f-8ef8-59b96e26a955" "answer-13a55cd8-59d4-51e6-bfc3-6f7e89d540fc" "answer-924475dc-6d5b-55d7-b419-2671e1182f9a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6a58b3c-76e3-54b6-ab35-4bf1be82b8db" :answer/id #uuid "d6a58b3c-76e3-54b6-ab35-4bf1be82b8db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-289a71e6-833a-5782-99bf-9eaaf974a1d9" :answer/id #uuid "289a71e6-833a-5782-99bf-9eaaf974a1d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-baaec9e1-8b5e-5f7f-8ef8-59b96e26a955" :answer/id #uuid "baaec9e1-8b5e-5f7f-8ef8-59b96e26a955" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13a55cd8-59d4-51e6-bfc3-6f7e89d540fc" :answer/id #uuid "13a55cd8-59d4-51e6-bfc3-6f7e89d540fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-924475dc-6d5b-55d7-b419-2671e1182f9a" :answer/id #uuid "924475dc-6d5b-55d7-b419-2671e1182f9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cdbbe1e3-c4b9-56df-b019-b8be69ef537e" :question/id #uuid "cdbbe1e3-c4b9-56df-b019-b8be69ef537e" :question/math-academy-id "q-74959" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{6+3}-3}{6+3-{3}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{6+y}-x)(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{6+y})^{2}-{x}^{2}}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{6+y-{x}^{2}}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+y}+x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+y}+x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+3}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{6}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eebddb13-5c72-52ae-80f4-9d372c2249ea"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eebddb13-5c72-52ae-80f4-9d372c2249ea" :answer-field/id #uuid "eebddb13-5c72-52ae-80f4-9d372c2249ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d4dd68e7-ba4b-5f9e-bb24-90eb7a550447" :answer-field/choices ["answer-47a24a05-5058-5973-b9b9-1f6b550f6f02" "answer-aa885501-8121-5a91-81dd-6ab6a0717534" "answer-d4dd68e7-ba4b-5f9e-bb24-90eb7a550447" "answer-3da1bffe-a256-5463-b543-084468d49c2f" "answer-5512019f-2424-5d94-8ce3-00141bd0391c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47a24a05-5058-5973-b9b9-1f6b550f6f02" :answer/id #uuid "47a24a05-5058-5973-b9b9-1f6b550f6f02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aa885501-8121-5a91-81dd-6ab6a0717534" :answer/id #uuid "aa885501-8121-5a91-81dd-6ab6a0717534" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4dd68e7-ba4b-5f9e-bb24-90eb7a550447" :answer/id #uuid "d4dd68e7-ba4b-5f9e-bb24-90eb7a550447" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3da1bffe-a256-5463-b543-084468d49c2f" :answer/id #uuid "3da1bffe-a256-5463-b543-084468d49c2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5512019f-2424-5d94-8ce3-00141bd0391c" :answer/id #uuid "5512019f-2424-5d94-8ce3-00141bd0391c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6e200150-12cc-5141-a4cc-a34826db19ce" :question/id #uuid "6e200150-12cc-5141-a4cc-a34826db19ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-74962" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2}=\\frac{2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{3+(-3)+4}-2}=\\frac{6-6}{\\sqrt{4}-2}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{zx+zy}{\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+y+4}-2)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{(\\sqrt{x+y+4})^{2}-{2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{(zx+zy)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{x+y+4-4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,\\frac{z(x+y)(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)}{x+y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,z(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,-3,2)}\\,z(\\sqrt{x+y+4}+2) & =2(\\sqrt{3-3+4}+2) \\\\  & =2(\\sqrt{4}+2) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d6b9cba1-39d8-5078-b34a-2ad5eaf58ff1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d6b9cba1-39d8-5078-b34a-2ad5eaf58ff1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d6b9cba1-39d8-5078-b34a-2ad5eaf58ff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-40144898-c856-5d2a-8c25-56d7a6e1fd50" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd3eb38d-a93e-535b-a33a-1db8c0313fe8" "answer-3a28c3c9-2e36-51fc-96e3-067a7a2ef398" "answer-15697aa4-8790-5d84-b361-1fcd5c853cfd" "answer-08e5ed22-82cd-5dd4-ae2b-e1265c44598a" "answer-40144898-c856-5d2a-8c25-56d7a6e1fd50"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd3eb38d-a93e-535b-a33a-1db8c0313fe8" :answer/id #uuid "dd3eb38d-a93e-535b-a33a-1db8c0313fe8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a28c3c9-2e36-51fc-96e3-067a7a2ef398" :answer/id #uuid "3a28c3c9-2e36-51fc-96e3-067a7a2ef398" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15697aa4-8790-5d84-b361-1fcd5c853cfd" :answer/id #uuid "15697aa4-8790-5d84-b361-1fcd5c853cfd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08e5ed22-82cd-5dd4-ae2b-e1265c44598a" :answer/id #uuid "08e5ed22-82cd-5dd4-ae2b-e1265c44598a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40144898-c856-5d2a-8c25-56d7a6e1fd50" :answer/id #uuid "40144898-c856-5d2a-8c25-56d7a6e1fd50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9df4444a-990b-5f68-bec9-ae4f2c45450b" :question/id #uuid "9df4444a-990b-5f68-bec9-ae4f2c45450b" :question/math-academy-id "q-129402" :question/problem "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{({0}^{2})(0)}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}(0)}{{x}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-78bd852f-26bb-5b2b-90e2-b2dc4782250d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-78bd852f-26bb-5b2b-90e2-b2dc4782250d" :answer-field/id #uuid "78bd852f-26bb-5b2b-90e2-b2dc4782250d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f1888faf-1d96-547d-a447-76ace2c376d4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ae730908-c126-5126-80c5-7ba1a6367fcb" "answer-90465371-f2d3-501a-b599-455e14d6af8d" "answer-753869fe-932f-5db8-83a0-37c1015d805e" "answer-0924a936-e922-5c55-aa02-d684eeb3f2b8" "answer-f1888faf-1d96-547d-a447-76ace2c376d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ae730908-c126-5126-80c5-7ba1a6367fcb" :answer/id #uuid "ae730908-c126-5126-80c5-7ba1a6367fcb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90465371-f2d3-501a-b599-455e14d6af8d" :answer/id #uuid "90465371-f2d3-501a-b599-455e14d6af8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-753869fe-932f-5db8-83a0-37c1015d805e" :answer/id #uuid "753869fe-932f-5db8-83a0-37c1015d805e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0924a936-e922-5c55-aa02-d684eeb3f2b8" :answer/id #uuid "0924a936-e922-5c55-aa02-d684eeb3f2b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1888faf-1d96-547d-a447-76ace2c376d4" :answer/id #uuid "f1888faf-1d96-547d-a447-76ace2c376d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186380062 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nI. The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n\nII. The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y={x}^{2}$\n\nIII. The limit exists and is equal to $1$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186335802 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{2y+x}{3x+y}\n$$\n\nI. The limit evaluates to $0$ along the path $y=x$\n\nII. The limit evaluates to $-\\frac{1}{2}$ along the path $y=-x$\n\nIII. The limit exists and is equal to $0$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125731 :tutorial/content "We can extend our knowledge of limits to multivariable functions. We'll first consider the two-variable case. \n\nWe denote $\\mathbf{x}=(x,y)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_0 = (a,b)$ as a particular, fixed vector. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined in some neighborhood of $\\mathbf x_0.$ \n\nThen, the notation\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to \\mathbf{x}_0} f (x,y) = L\n$$\n\nmeans that the value of $f$ can be made as close as we want to $L,$ provided that $\\mathbf x$ is sufficiently close to $\\mathbf{x}_0.$\n\nA few things to note:\n\n- This definition of a limit also extends to functions of more than two variables in the natural way.\n\n- All of the rules set out by the algebra of limits are valid for functions of several variables.\n\nWe say that $f(x,y)$ is **continuous** at $(a,b)$ if\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\mathbf{x} \\to (a,b)} f (x,y) = f(a,b).\n$$\n\nSo, if a function is continuous at $(a,b),$ then the limit can be evaluated by simply plugging $(x,y) = (a,b)$ into the function.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}\n$$\n\nThis function is continuous for all $(x,y)$ with the exception of $(0,0)$ where the denominator vanishes. Since the point $(2,1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(x,y) = (2,1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124724 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
